# ✅ What are SOLID Principles?

**SOLID** is an acronym for **5 object-oriented design principles** that help you write:

✔ Maintainable code
✔ Scalable systems
✔ Testable components
✔ Less buggy software

They are:

| Letter | Principle             | Meaning                                              |
| ------ | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| S      | Single Responsibility | One class = one job                                  |
| O      | Open/Closed           | Open for extension, closed for modification          |
| L      | Liskov Substitution   | Subclasses should replace parent without breaking    |
| I      | Interface Segregation | Don’t force unused methods                           |
| D      | Dependency Inversion  | Depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations |

---

# 1️⃣ S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

### 👉 A class/module should have ONLY ONE reason to change

❌ Bad Example (doing too much):

```js
class UserService {
  createUser(user) {
    // create user
  }

  sendEmail(user) {
    // send welcome email
  }

  logUser(user) {
    // logging logic
  }
}
```

⚠️ Problems:

* User logic
* Email logic
* Logging logic
  👉 3 responsibilities = bad design

---

✅ Good Example:

```js
class UserService {
  createUser(user) {
    // create user
  }
}

class EmailService {
  sendWelcomeEmail(user) {
    // send email
  }
}

class Logger {
  log(data) {
    // logging
  }
}
```

### ✅ Benefits:

* Easier testing
* Easier changes
* Cleaner code

---

# 2️⃣ O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP)

### 👉 Code should be open for extension, but closed for modification

❌ Bad:

```js
function calculateDiscount(type, amount) {
  if (type === "student") {
    return amount * 0.8;
  }
  if (type === "senior") {
    return amount * 0.7;
  }
}
```

👉 Every new discount = modify function

---

✅ Good:

```js
class Discount {
  apply(amount) {}
}

class StudentDiscount extends Discount {
  apply(amount) {
    return amount * 0.8;
  }
}

class SeniorDiscount extends Discount {
  apply(amount) {
    return amount * 0.7;
  }
}
```

Usage:

```js
function calculate(discountStrategy, amount) {
  return discountStrategy.apply(amount);
}
```

👉 Add new discount without changing old code

---

# 3️⃣ L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

### 👉 Child class should behave like parent class without breaking logic

❌ Bad classic example:

```js
class Bird {
  fly() {}
}

class Penguin extends Bird {
  fly() {
    throw new Error("Penguins can't fly");
  }
}
```

⚠️ Penguin breaks expectation of Bird

---

✅ Good:

```js
class Bird {}

class FlyingBird extends Bird {
  fly() {}
}

class Penguin extends Bird {
  swim() {}
}
```

👉 Now no broken behavior

---

# 4️⃣ I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

(JS doesn’t have interfaces but concept applies)

### 👉 Don’t force classes to implement methods they don’t use

❌ Bad:

```js
class Worker {
  work() {}
  eat() {}
}

class Robot extends Worker {
  eat() { 
    throw new Error("Robots don't eat");
  }
}
```

---

✅ Good:

```js
class Workable {
  work() {}
}

class Eatable {
  eat() {}
}

class Human extends Workable {
  eat() {}
}

class Robot extends Workable {}
```

👉 Smaller focused contracts

---

# 5️⃣ D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

### 👉 High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules

### 👉 Both depend on abstractions

❌ Bad:

```js
class MySQLDatabase {
  connect() {}
}

class UserRepository {
  constructor() {
    this.db = new MySQLDatabase(); // tightly coupled
  }
}
```

---

✅ Good:

```js
class Database {
  connect() {}
}

class MySQLDatabase extends Database {
  connect() {}
}

class UserRepository {
  constructor(database) {
    this.db = database;
  }
}
```

Usage:

```js
const db = new MySQLDatabase();
const repo = new UserRepository(db);
```

👉 Easy to swap DB later (Mongo, Firebase, etc.)

